  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Identify whether it's sin, cos or tan from the equation",
      "Know the key features: amplitude, period, asymptotes",
      "Use exact values at key angles (0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°)",
      "For reading graphs: find angle on x-axis, read across to curve, down to y-axis",
      "Remember: sin and cos repeat every 360°, tan repeats every 180°"
    ],
    "title": "How to Sketch and Use Trigonometric Graphs",
    "content": "<p>The three trigonometric graphs you need are <strong>sin</strong>, <strong>cos</strong> and <strong>tan</strong>.</p><p>\\(y = \\sin x\\) is a wave oscillating between −1 and 1, crossing the x-axis at 0°, 180°, 360°. Maximum at 90°, minimum at 270°. Period = 360°.</p><p>\\(y = \\cos x\\) is the same wave shifted left by 90°. It starts at 1 when \\(x = 0°\\), crosses at 90° and 270°, minimum at 180°. Period = 360°.</p><p>\\(y = \\tan x\\) has vertical asymptotes at 90°, 270° etc. It has period 180° and passes through the origin. The curve goes from −∞ to +∞ between each pair of asymptotes.</p><p>Know exact values: \\(\\sin 30° = 0.5\\), \\(\\cos 60° = 0.5\\), \\(\\tan 45° = 1\\), \\(\\sin 90° = 1\\), \\(\\cos 0° = 1\\).</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>Find \\(\\sin 30°\\) and \\(\\cos 60°\\).</strong></p><p>From exact values: \\(\\sin 30° = 0.5\\) and \\(\\cos 60° = 0.5\\).</p><p>Notice they are equal — this is because \\(\\sin x = \\cos(90° - x)\\).</p>"
  },
